  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nMost of central and southeast IL will remain under a very warm air \nmass causing highs to reach the upper 80s to lower 90s again today, \nlikely breaking more daily temperature records. Meanwhile, a cold \nfront will nudge into the vicinity of Peoria, limiting temperatures \nto a few degrees cooler from Peoria northward. The cold front will \nlikely remain dry through the afternoon in the absence of \nsubstantial lift, although low level frontogenesis just to the west \nalong the front could bring a close call for some morning shower \nactivity north of Peoria.\n\nThunderstorms look to develop along the front over the front from KS \nthrough MO in the afternoon today as a shortwave tracks along the \nfront coincident with increased moisture, MLCAPE up to around 2000 \nJ/kg, and around 40 kt deep layer shear. This area of thunderstorms \nshould move and grow eastward, with high-res models depicting storms \nin the Illinois River Valley withing a few hours after sunset. \nModels decrease CAPE, especially surface/ML CAPE as thunderstorms \nmove into central IL, however 1000-15000 MUCAPE maintains through \nTuesday night. This should result in isolated severe storm \npotential, and SPC depicts a Marginal Risk in much of central IL as \nfar east as I-57 for a threat of locally damaging wind gusts to 60 \nmph, as well as a threat for 1\"+ hail eastward to around the IL \nRiver.\n\nAs the front stalls south of the area Wednesday, multiple rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms are depicted to continue across the area, \nalthough with many breaks in activity as well. A strong surface low \nwill finally track through the area Thursday, bringing a likelihood \nof widespread rain, likely the heaviest of the midweek period. This \nsystem will bring a return of strong deep layer shear and moderate \ninstability that could result in severe thunderstorms again. SPC \ndepicts a Marginal Risk for much of the area southeast of the IL \nRiver. Model blend brings around 1 to 1.5 inches of\nprecipitation north of I-70 through Thursday, with around 0.75 \ninches to the south. Nevertheless, some isolated 2+ inch \ndownpours can be expected with thunderstorm activity as shown by\nseveral high-res models, and this could be enough for some \nlocally excessive rainfall, despite recent dry conditions. WPC \nhas a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall over portions of the \narea Tuesday through Thursday. With cloud cover and periods of \nrain as well as a cold front located just south of the area, \ntemperatures should be down considerably Wednesday and \nThursday, with highs likely to be in lower to mid 70s north of \nI-72 to upper 70s or lower 80s to the south.\n\nBehind Thursday's low a strong cold front will sweep through \nthe area, bringing much cooler and dry air by Friday. Models \nhave converged on a much narrower range of temperatures than the\npast few days, with 10th-90th percentile range only around 10 \ndegrees. This should result in Highs Friday and Saturday around \n60 (plus or minus 5 degrees), and lows mid to upper 30s Friday \nnight and Saturday night (plus or minus 5 degrees). With the \nhigh perhaps directly overhead Saturday morning, this would be \nthe best chance for frost or freezing conditions.\n\nAs high pressure moves off to the east Sunday, a strong return of \nwarm air will bring a warming trend through early next week, \nresulting in highs rebounding to nearly 70 degrees Sunday, \nlower to mid 70s Monday, and mid to upper 70s Tuesday.",
